(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 42-year-old man whose two children developed hot tub folliculitis last month asks how to stop it happening again. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Shower thoroughly within thirty minutes of leaving the water on every occasion","Raise the water temperature above forty degrees to kill the responsible organism","Limit each session to twenty minutes and avoid submerging the trunk entirely","Maintain continuous water filtration, monitor disinfectant levels and change the water often"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Showering after contact does not prevent the infection. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man is admitted with necrotizing fasciitis after dental sepsis. Which referrals should be arranged?","choices":["Dermatology alone, since the presenting problem is a skin infection","Infectious diseases alone, since antibiotic selection determines outcome","Wound care nursing alone, with review by the general team each day","A team approach with surgical intensive care, ideally at a burn or trauma centre"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — consultations are described as necessary rather than optional. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"A 18-year-old man with severe nodular acne has failed a topical retinoid with an oral antibiotic and benzoyl peroxide. Which test is required before the next treatment is started?","choices":["A fasting insulin and lipid panel, to identify the insulin resistance driving the acne","A skin biopsy of a nodular lesion, to exclude an alternative inflammatory disease","A pregnancy test is not needed for him, but iPledge enrolment of the prescriber is required","A bacterial culture of a nodule, to confirm that Cutibacterium acnes is still present"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — isotretinoin requires prescriber enrolment and a one-month supply at a time. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with erysipelas of the face has no drug allergies. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Cephalexin, with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole added if purulence develops later","Mupirocin ointment, with oral dicloxacillin if the lesions become widespread","Clindamycin, which is reserved for patients who report a penicillin allergy","Penicillin V, with symptomatic care, hydration and elevation of the affected part"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — prompt treatment matters because progression can be rapid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 47-year-old healthy woman has a small area of cellulitis on the forearm with minimal pain, no fever and no risk factors for serious illness. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Plain films of the forearm, since underlying osteomyelitis must be excluded","Venous duplex ultrasound, since deep vein thrombosis must be excluded first","No workup, since the diagnosis is clinical in limited disease without systemic signs","Blood cultures and a punch biopsy, since organism identification guides therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serious infection warrants blood cultures, punch biopsy, blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman who had a mastectomy with axillary node clearance has recurrent erysipelas of that arm. Which factor most explains the recurrence?","choices":["Traumatic inoculation of bacteria into the dermis","Obstruction of the apocrine ducts in the axilla","Impaired lymphatic drainage from the node clearance","Nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus after surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is the first risk factor named for erysipelas. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who works outdoors in humid weather has small pustules on an erythematous base across the thighs and trunk, each pierced by a central hair. He is afebrile and otherwise well. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial folliculitis","Acne vulgaris of the trunk","Miliaria from heat and occlusion","Non-bullous impetigo of the trunk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a follicular pustule pierced by a central hair, with no systemic involvement. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has early acute paronychia with erythema and tenderness but no fluctuance. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Warm water soaks for twenty minutes three times daily","Incision and drainage under digital block anaesthesia","A broad-spectrum topical antifungal applied twice daily","Immediate removal of the nail plate under local block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Incision and drainage is reserved for severe cases with purulent collection. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with suspected necrotizing fasciitis is febrile and hypotensive. The surgical team is available now. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Complete computed tomography of the limb before any operative decision is taken","Await blood culture results before committing the patient to an operation","Trial forty-eight hours of broad intravenous antibiotics and then reassess","Proceed to surgical debridement, since tests and imaging must not delay intervention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is a surgical emergency with high mortality. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman started on antibiotics for cellulitis yesterday reports the leg looks worse today, though she feels no more unwell. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Arrange urgent surgical review, because worsening at any point suggests necrotizing fasciitis","Reassure her, because sudden destruction of pathogens releases enzymes that increase local inflammation","Change the antibiotic immediately, because worsening on day one indicates the wrong organism","Admit her for intravenous therapy, because worsening at any point signals systemic spread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fever usually resolves in twenty-four hours and inflammation over one to two weeks. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has confirmed necrotizing fasciitis. Which combination of care is required?","choices":["Bedside incision and drainage followed by discharge on oral antibiotic therapy","Elevation with warm compresses and a narrow-spectrum oral antibiotic course","Aggressive surgical debridement, broad antibiotic cover and surgical intensive care admission","Broad intravenous antibiotics with observation on a general ward for forty-eight hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cover aerobic Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms and anaerobes, with a team approach. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with acne asks how soon she should expect to see a difference. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Improvement is immediate, and any delay indicates the regimen should be changed","Improvement takes four to six weeks, and the back and chest may take three to four months","Improvement takes one to two weeks, and all affected sites respond at the same rate","Improvement takes six to eight months, and the face is the slowest site to respond"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clinical improvement is judged by new lesion count at six to eight weeks. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has a punched-out ulcer on the shin with a thick grey-yellow crust that has been present for two weeks and is healing slowly. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Venous ulceration of the shin","Ecthyma","Non-bullous impetigo","Pyoderma gangrenosum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a deeper impetigo that ulcerates into the dermis, heals slowly and leaves a scar. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 70-year-old immunocompromised man has extensive cellulitis of the leg with systemic signs. Which investigations are appropriate?","choices":["Blood cultures, punch biopsy, complete blood count and creatine phosphokinase","No investigations, since cellulitis is a clinical diagnosis in every patient","Wood's lamp examination and a potassium hydroxide preparation of the skin","Nasal swab for staphylococcal carriage and a plucked hair for dermatophytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with imaging considered for underlying fasciitis or osteomyelitis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy has open and closed comedones across the forehead and nose, with no papules or pustules. He has used only over-the-counter face wash. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A topical retinoid, switching to azelaic or salicylic acid if not tolerated","An oral tetracycline with topical benzoyl peroxide added to limit resistance","Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy over a sixteen to twenty week course","A combined oral contraceptive pill to reduce ovarian androgen production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the guideline entry point for comedonal acne. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has scarring across both cheeks that seems far more extensive than her current lesion count would explain. Which should be suspected?","choices":["Inadequate duration of treatment for the back and the chest","Manipulation of the lesions by picking or squeezing","Resistance of Cutibacterium acnes to her topical antibiotic","An undiagnosed hyperandrogenic state driving deeper lesions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the specific inference the lecture draws. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman develops itchy follicular papules and pustules across the trunk and buttocks two days after using a hotel hot tub. Her face, palms and soles are spared. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pseudomonas folliculitis","Staphylococcal folliculitis","Contact dermatitis from pool chemicals","Miliaria from heat and occlusive clothing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the timing, the distribution and the sparing of face, neck, soles and palms all fit. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has cellulitis of the thigh with a central area of purulence. Which antibiotic choice is most appropriate?","choices":["Penicillin V, with clindamycin if the patient is penicillin allergic","Topical mupirocin, with oral cephalexin if lesions become widespread","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, clindamycin or linezolid","Dicloxacillin or cephalexin, with clindamycin if penicillin allergic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — purulent cellulitis prompts cover for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A parent asks whether antibiotics given earlier would have prevented their child's post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Antibiotics prevent it only when the causative organism has been confirmed by culture","Antibiotics worsen it, which is why topical therapy is preferred in young children","Antibiotics do not prevent it, because the immune activation usually precedes treatment","Antibiotics prevent it reliably if started within the first forty-eight hours of the rash"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is stated explicitly for this complication. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with pseudofolliculitis barbae must remain clean-shaven for work. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Shave daily against the direction of growth so the hairs are cut below the skin surface","Use the closest-cutting multi-blade razor available to reduce the frequency of shaving","Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical corticosteroid","Use a single or double blade at a mild angle, avoid lift-and-cut systems, and keep razors clean"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with chemical depilatories and laser hair removal as alternatives. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who smokes has had painful nodules and draining sinuses in both axillae and the groin for two years, flaring five or six times a year, with meshlike scars between them. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Recurrent furunculosis of the axilla","Cutaneous Crohn's disease of the groin","Erythrasma of the axilla and the groin","Hidradenitis suppurativa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — typical lesions, apocrine distribution and recurrence more than twice in six months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with diabetes has extensive folliculitis and a culture growing methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which oral options are appropriate?","choices":["Doxycycline, minocycline or sarecycline for acne","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or linezolid","Cephalexin, dicloxacillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate","Penicillin V, erythromycin base or clarithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin and dicloxacillin cover the methicillin-sensitive organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has an axillary abscess that has not drained spontaneously. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Wide excision of the axilla","Surgical incision and drainage","Warm soaks and observation","Oral antibiotics on their own"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If a lesion drains on its own, warm soaks with broad-spectrum antibiotics are used. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy with untreated acne develops tender fluctuant nodules with purulent drainage over the same areas. Which best describes what has happened?","choices":["A foreign body reaction to hair has developed within the affected follicles","A secondary bacterial infection has developed within skin already affected by acne","A primary bacterial infection has arisen in previously normal and intact skin","The acne has progressed to its nodular form without any additional infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — secondary infection arises in skin damaged by an existing condition. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with suspected necrotizing fasciitis of the thigh has a computed tomography scan showing gas in the soft tissue fascial planes. Which organism does this suggest?","choices":["Group A streptococcus, since gas is not present with Clostridium perfringens","Staphylococcus aureus, which produces gas through epidermolytic toxins","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which produces gas in inadequately perfused tissue","Clostridium perfringens, since gas is not present with Group A streptococcus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ultrasound also demonstrates air bubbles in soft tissue. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a single painful fluctuant nodule 8 mm across on the back of the neck with a single central opening. He is afebrile. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Incision and drainage with culture, plus an oral antibiotic because the lesion is over 5 mm","Warm compresses alone, since a single afebrile lesion never requires any antibiotic","Oral antibiotics alone, since incising a furuncle risks seeding the bloodstream","Wide surgical excision of the affected area to prevent the lesion recurring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a single lesion over 5 mm is one of the stated antibiotic indications. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"The parent of a 6-year-old with impetigo asks when he can go back to school. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["He may return after ten full days from the appearance of the very first lesion","He may return once treatment has been underway for twenty-four to forty-eight hours","He may return once every crusted lesion has completely healed and the skin is clear","He may return immediately, since impetigo does not spread from one person to another"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths and razors must not be shared. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has erythrasma confined to both inguinal folds. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Topical miconazole cream applied twice daily","Topical mupirocin ointment applied three times daily","Topical erythromycin or clindamycin","Oral erythromycin or clarithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oral erythromycin or clarithromycin is used for widespread disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy had impetigo three weeks ago. He now has puffy eyelids, tea-coloured urine and a blood pressure of 138/88. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","Acute rheumatic fever after streptococcal infection","Secondary bacterial infection of the healing lesions","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome in evolution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — sudden oedema, haematuria, proteinuria and hypertension, especially at three to seven years. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 44-year-old bartender has had swollen, tender nail folds on several fingers for four months, with thickened discoloured nails and cuticles that have separated from the nail plate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic paronychia","Acute paronychia","Onychomycosis","Nail psoriasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an inflammatory reaction to irritants over at least six weeks, with Candida albicans commonest. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 122"}]};
